WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.120
It's great to welcome back to the program
today.

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:06.031
Jonathon hight, who is a social psychologist
and professor at [inaudible]

00:00:06.031 --> 00:00:08.510
New York University Stern School of business.

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:13.200
He's the author of multiple New York Times
bestsellers, including the righteous mind.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.770
The newer book coauthored with Greg Luke.

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:21.210
Ian Off the coddling of the American mind
is just now available in paperback.

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:23.199
Jonathan, so great to have you back on.

00:00:23.199 --> 00:00:24.199
Thanks.

00:00:24.199 --> 00:00:25.320
David's great to be back.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:31.710
So we've been talking a lot on the program
about the, um, sort of media cycles related

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:36.480
to kind of modern politics now in particular
as the election is starting to ramp up and

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:43.090
how sort of at a core, structural level, the
way in which corporate media cover politics

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:48.100
perpetuates a lot of the polarization that
you've been writing about for a long time.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:50.040
Do you see it that way today?

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:51.700
Oh my goodness, yes.

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:58.309
Um, so, you know, I, I think of human beings
as this amazing species that that came down

00:00:58.309 --> 00:01:01.850
out of, out of the trees and evolved for life
in small groups.

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:07.850
Um, you know, groups of 50 to 150 people that
are constantly, or not constantly, but often

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:11.749
at war with each other, or at least having
violent intergroup conflict.

00:01:11.749 --> 00:01:16.840
Um, and we, uh, and we evolve to circle around
rocks and trees and other things, make them

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.530
sacred and then have small scale religion.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:20.530
That's human nature.

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:23.329
Now somehow or other, we escaped from that.

00:01:23.329 --> 00:01:28.060
We built these gigantic societies, uh, that
were feudal originally and very hierarchical.

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:33.979
And then very recently we built these amazing
secular it get relatively egalitarian, open,

00:01:33.979 --> 00:01:35.389
prosperous democracies.

00:01:35.389 --> 00:01:38.969
Uh, really quite amazing what we've done.

00:01:38.969 --> 00:01:46.679
But we're still the same tribal religious
creatures that we've always been and the modern

00:01:46.679 --> 00:01:52.270
media ecosystem beginning with cable TV and
then moving onto the Internet and social media

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:57.209
has gotten more and more effective at breaking
us up into smaller groups, feeding us with

00:01:57.209 --> 00:01:58.359
very different fats.

00:01:58.359 --> 00:02:03.469
We live in different worlds, a worlds in which
we, we hate the people and the other worlds.

00:02:03.469 --> 00:02:06.350
Um, and we increasingly are willing to take
action against them.

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:08.129
So yes, it's very frightening.

00:02:08.129 --> 00:02:14.450
One of the criticisms that sometimes levied
when one starts to make a more general, we

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:19.590
live in different worlds and have different
facts sort of analysis, which is not an a

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:20.590
wrong analysis.

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:25.650
But one of the criticisms that sometimes comes
up is yes, people live in these self perpetuating,

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:32.629
Algorithmic Echo chamber sort of worlds with
different facts, but there is still some truth.

00:02:32.629 --> 00:02:36.040
There are things that are true and things
that are not true.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:41.980
And there needs to be more of a maybe reckoning,
some would say, which separates the fact that

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:47.109
there is a degree of polarization in which
people are just an echo chambers.

00:02:47.109 --> 00:02:52.200
And at the same time there are, there is truth
and falsity at the same time as they're not.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:56.079
Oh, well, I certainly agree that there is
truth or falsity, but I don't see that as

00:02:56.079 --> 00:03:00.709
a, as a critique or criticism of the view
that we now live in separate worlds.

00:03:00.709 --> 00:03:04.700
Rather, I would say the truth has always been
hard to find.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:09.480
Um, even about medical issues, people didn't
notice that if, you know, doctors wash their

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:11.180
hands, their patients don't die.

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:14.200
Uh, and even when that was pointed out to
them, they didn't believe it.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.450
So it's very hard to find the truth.

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:20.450
Uh, we have universities, we have an academic
system to ferret it out, and we also have

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:22.220
journalists who are supposed to do that.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:24.840
So we have adaptations to help us find the
truth.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:30.120
But man, has it gotten harder in the last
five or 10 years because of social media?

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:31.629
Almost anything that you take as a fact.

00:03:31.629 --> 00:03:33.830
Now, uh, there are people on the other side.

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:38.639
If it has a political valence, there are people
on the other side who, who are certain and

00:03:38.639 --> 00:03:40.099
have evidence that you're wrong.

00:03:40.099 --> 00:03:44.670
Um, and because I'm in the center and I write
about these things, I get emails from people

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:45.780
on all sides.

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:49.680
So whatever the scandal, whether it's Brett
Kavanaugh, whether it's the Amazon rainforest

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:54.220
burning, um, people send me things to show,
oh look, the other side's crazy.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:55.220
You know?

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:58.159
Uh, so, um, so no, I do agree that there's
truth.

00:03:58.159 --> 00:04:04.170
That's why I'm a researcher and a professor,
but I am despairing of our ability as a country

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:07.029
to find truth on important matters ever again.

00:04:07.029 --> 00:04:13.170
So what is the, is there a set of a, of a
ta, I dunno, this set of steps, so to speak,

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:18.699
for someone who wants to determine that truth
because as you say, there's a, there's a significant

00:04:18.699 --> 00:04:21.060
difference between supply side.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:25.380
Economics is a positive or negative influence
on our, on our economy for example.

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:31.680
And, uh, here is the reality of what we are
doing to the planet because of industrialization

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:32.970
and the burning of fossil fuels.

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:36.669
I see those as as distinctly different sorts
of arguments.

00:04:36.669 --> 00:04:40.000
Question one is do you agree that those are
different types of arguments?

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:43.990
And then the maybe more important part is
what is the process you can recommend to people

00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:47.770
to arrive at the truth about either or both
of those issues?

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:52.979
Well, it's true that there are certain propositions
that are more easily testable and so on.

00:04:52.979 --> 00:04:55.610
The question is the planet warming.

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:59.460
But there are some people in the right who
deny that, but as far as I can tell there

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:00.460
are very few.

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:05.370
So that I think is widely accepted as a fact
and has been for a long time, not by all.

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:09.000
Um, next question, are human beings responsible
for the warming?

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:12.660
Well then some more people on the right will
drop off and say they used to say no, it's

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:13.660
sunspots.

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:15.090
I don't know if they still say that.

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:19.220
Um, so there are certain things that are fact,
and I certainly think it's established that

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:21.750
human activity is the major cause of the rise.

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:25.650
Uh, but then the question is, uh, anything
else that happens?

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:29.490
So let's just take the, the fires and the
Amazon that people are understandably worried

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:30.490
about.

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:34.800
Well, if you're on the lift, you're told that
this is, that this, the Amazon is the lungs

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:37.820
of the world, makes 20% of our oxygen.

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:42.370
Uh, but I read from people on the right end
in the center, uh, that biologist say that

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:43.370
that's not true.

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:44.849
I know I haven't dug into it.

00:05:44.849 --> 00:05:45.849
I don't know.

00:05:45.849 --> 00:05:49.740
I'm just saying that, um, and you're a counter
point.

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:51.770
Well, it's the, and it's not just that there
was a counterpoint.

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:58.159
Uh, it's that we, if you're a partisan, if
you're on a team, whatever comes your way,

00:05:58.159 --> 00:06:00.160
you don't just say, hmm, is it true?

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:05.379
You say, Oh my God, this confirms the things
that I believe most desperately.

00:06:05.379 --> 00:06:07.729
This confirms why they're so evil.

00:06:07.729 --> 00:06:10.349
And you have lots of people helping you confirm
that.

00:06:10.349 --> 00:06:12.389
It's called confirmation bias.

00:06:12.389 --> 00:06:15.050
That we don't say, well what's the evidence
on both sides?

00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:20.280
We say, can I, can I take this as evidence
that my enemies are evil?

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:23.550
We look for that evidence and we always find
it and the internet just Google it, you'll

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:25.900
always, you'll find it online.

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:30.620
So yeah, there are facts, but I think, um,
I am again, I am just despairing of our ability

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:32.490
to find them.

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:40.639
You have talked about, um, the sort of, um,
evolutionary advantages, some of the dynamics

00:06:40.639 --> 00:06:46.949
that we observe in humans, uh, have had at
prior times and maybe even still have today.

00:06:46.949 --> 00:06:52.849
One of the things I've been studying is this
concept of the narcissism of small differences.

00:06:52.849 --> 00:06:58.350
And I'm, I'm curious if we imagine a world
where imagined that the right convinced the

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:04.050
left to come to their side on global warming,
on abortion, on gay men, whatever, right?

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:06.370
Let's say the left said, you know what, you've
convinced us.

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:07.460
You're right.

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:15.800
Uh, would there not be a re stratification
within the new sort of pillars of discussion

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:22.229
wherein there would be a new outgroup within
the right that would be on the left of what

00:07:22.229 --> 00:07:23.229
is left.

00:07:23.229 --> 00:07:24.229
So to speak.

00:07:24.229 --> 00:07:28.330
And you would have the same exact degree of
polarization, disagreement and sort of alternate

00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:30.260
fact worlds that you describe.

00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:31.260
Yes.

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:32.260
In general.

00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:33.260
I think that's true.

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:35.090
I mean, we certainly have made a lot of moral
progress.

00:07:35.090 --> 00:07:37.639
Each side has convinced the other a few things.

00:07:37.639 --> 00:07:42.879
The direction of history has been more in
the progressive direction in that, um, uh,

00:07:42.879 --> 00:07:45.150
you know, should women have the right to vote?

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:48.659
Should women have equal opportunity or equal
access to everything?

00:07:48.659 --> 00:07:52.259
Uh, there's not any serious debate or doubt
over that.

00:07:52.259 --> 00:07:57.479
So then we move on where the left says, well,
okay, um, now what we need is equal representation.

00:07:57.479 --> 00:08:03.289
That women should be 50% of say all the, you
know, all the s all the engineers and computer

00:08:03.289 --> 00:08:06.250
programmers and the rights says, no, we should
have equal opportunity.

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:09.680
Um, I wouldn't exactly call it the narcissism
of small differences.

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:12.610
Those are major and important philosophical
questions.

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:15.180
What counts as the standard for a fair society?

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:20.449
Um, but yes, I think your general point is
that progress, uh, progress, be it material

00:08:20.449 --> 00:08:24.280
progress or moral progress will never end.

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:26.509
Are, are furious debates.

00:08:26.509 --> 00:08:32.990
We will always find things to argue about
something as simple as do we need the, is

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:36.720
the cause of our problems that we've lost
touch with the old ways and the wisdom of

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:41.740
our ancestors or is the problem, uh, that
we are not advancing fast enough and we need

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:44.730
to tear down what we've inherited and build
something new?

00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:47.970
That's the fundamental question between progressives
and conservatives.

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:54.260
Is the political system that we have a well-suited
to adjudicate some of these bigger questions

00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:59.110
that you've mentioned today and also that
you talk about in the book or is it fundamentally

00:08:59.110 --> 00:09:05.550
a system that either by design or by outcome
by side effect is not going to possibly resolve

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:06.550
those disagreements?

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:11.040
Yeah, well, I would never say not possibly
because the future is very long and we've

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:12.700
been through bad times before.

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:17.750
So, uh, you know, 50 a hundred years from
now, I have no idea where we'll be.

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:19.570
My friend Steven Pinker's probably right.

00:09:19.570 --> 00:09:20.880
That things will be a lot better.

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:22.690
Certainly on material issues they'll be a
lot better.

00:09:22.690 --> 00:09:28.510
But are there specifically some design flaws
or problems with our system that make us worse

00:09:28.510 --> 00:09:34.040
or more gridlocked, more, um, dysfunctional
than many other democracies?

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:35.520
I think the answer is yes.

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:41.510
Um, so the worst number of political parties
to have in a country is one that's really,

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:42.640
really bad.

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:48.030
Um, but the second worst number is to, uh,
because our minds are really finely tuned

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:49.850
to do us versus them.

00:09:49.850 --> 00:09:53.590
And there's even research showing that if
you break people up into groups into three

00:09:53.590 --> 00:09:55.760
or four different groups, you don't get the
kind of split.

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:59.690
You don't get the ferocity of the split that
you get when you break people up into two

00:09:59.690 --> 00:10:00.690
groups.

00:10:00.690 --> 00:10:02.710
So two is a, we have two parties.

00:10:02.710 --> 00:10:07.280
I wish we didn't, it wasn't the design of
the founders, but it emerged even over the

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:08.730
question of whether to ratify the constitution.

00:10:08.730 --> 00:10:10.300
There were the federalists and the anti-federalists.

00:10:10.300 --> 00:10:17.350
Um, our system is designed to make it easy
to block, uh, to block bad ideas, to block

00:10:17.350 --> 00:10:19.960
whims and passions of the public.

00:10:19.960 --> 00:10:24.080
So our system is designed to be at the top
chorusy and then various kinds of political

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:26.460
corruption have amplified that.

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:31.130
Any, any group that is strongly like the NRA,
any group that's really wants to stop something

00:10:31.130 --> 00:10:33.110
has a lot of ways they can stop it.

00:10:33.110 --> 00:10:37.230
Uh, whereas if the majority favor something,
it's very hard for them to actually push it

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:38.310
into law.

00:10:38.310 --> 00:10:44.660
What are the particular issues that you see
as, um, at least in the short to medium term,

00:10:44.660 --> 00:10:49.540
not solvable as a result of that structure?

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:55.540
Um, so I think, well, I think almost all of
our big issues are gonna be very hard to solve.

00:10:55.540 --> 00:10:58.540
So let's just take, uh, climate change for
one.

00:10:58.540 --> 00:11:02.950
Um, so I think the left has been correct about
the nature of the problem.

00:11:02.950 --> 00:11:08.990
Um, but the right has been correct that trying
to solve it by getting big government agreements

00:11:08.990 --> 00:11:09.990
is hopeless.

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:15.500
Uh, we're not going to get China and India,
um, to solve things because they agree, you

00:11:15.500 --> 00:11:17.610
know, to reduce the air pollution.

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:21.180
Uh, because of a, some agreement that their
government signed.

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:23.070
Emmanuel Macron tried to do that and he had
riots.

00:11:23.070 --> 00:11:26.720
He tried to, uh, have more, uh, environmental
minded policies.

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:28.220
He had riots in France.

00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:29.900
So countries aren't going to do that.

00:11:29.900 --> 00:11:33.130
Um, that way I think his failure lies failure.

00:11:33.130 --> 00:11:34.340
I think the right is correct.

00:11:34.340 --> 00:11:37.610
And here what I mean is especially the libertarian
or free market, right.

00:11:37.610 --> 00:11:43.290
Um, that these problems are massive and the
only way we're going to decarbonize is if

00:11:43.290 --> 00:11:49.810
individual consumers and companies conclude
that it's cheaper and better for them to use

00:11:49.810 --> 00:11:51.450
carbon free sources.

00:11:51.450 --> 00:11:58.540
And so, um, I think if we leave it to AOC
and the green new deal, um, which basically

00:11:58.540 --> 00:12:02.370
takes let's say tries to approach climate
change by importing all the things that the

00:12:02.370 --> 00:12:03.920
left most wants.

00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:08.150
That approach that we new deal has some, some
good things in it, but it guarantees that

00:12:08.150 --> 00:12:10.250
people on the right are going to fight it
tooth and nail.

00:12:10.250 --> 00:12:12.740
I mean we have to have a high minimum wage
to fight carbon.

00:12:12.740 --> 00:12:13.740
Yeah.

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:16.760
So, okay, but let's, let's go back just one,
one half a step maybe.

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:23.620
Um, I, I actually sorta sympathize with the
idea that I believe the fact bear out that

00:12:23.620 --> 00:12:29.080
in the long term it is actually cheaper, more
efficient than therefore more profitable to

00:12:29.080 --> 00:12:31.040
get away from fossil fuels.

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:38.440
But I think the, the missing step in the libertarian
approaches, unless there is an intermediary

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:45.380
force that pushes before we get to the longterm
in the short term, it's much easier not to

00:12:45.380 --> 00:12:46.380
do it.

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:49.500
And I think that that's where the libertarian
analysis that you made falls a little bit

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:50.500
short.

00:12:50.500 --> 00:12:51.500
Oh, totally agree.

00:12:51.500 --> 00:12:55.130
So, you know, if I were king or if I could
advise or if there was a government capable

00:12:55.130 --> 00:13:01.330
of acting on advice, um, I would say that,
um, that ultimately the heavy lifting is going

00:13:01.330 --> 00:13:03.640
to be done by market forces.

00:13:03.640 --> 00:13:09.810
And if we could have massive federal expenditure
into getting really cheap solar, wind and

00:13:09.810 --> 00:13:12.700
nuclear nuclear, it really has to be part
of the problem.

00:13:12.700 --> 00:13:17.890
If we're serious about this, um, massive government
expenditure to, to get to those points 10

00:13:17.890 --> 00:13:20.450
or 20 years earlier than we would without
subsidies.

00:13:20.450 --> 00:13:22.790
Yes, it's urgent that we do it.

00:13:22.790 --> 00:13:27.490
So I'm not a partisan, I'm not a conservative,
a progressive or libertarian.

00:13:27.490 --> 00:13:30.490
I think all three have, have insights.

00:13:30.490 --> 00:13:33.800
To go back to your early question, what should
one do if they want to find the truth?

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:35.580
I'll tell you what worked for me.

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:38.120
I was on the left when I started writing the
righteous mind.

00:13:38.120 --> 00:13:40.970
My goal was to help the Democrats win more
elections.

00:13:40.970 --> 00:13:43.480
And I committed myself to understanding conservatives
and Libertarians.

00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:48.970
And what I discovered is that if you look
at the best writing, you get lots of great

00:13:48.970 --> 00:13:49.970
ideas.

00:13:49.970 --> 00:13:52.020
There are lots of really smart, interesting
people on all sides.

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:56.180
Um, whereas if you get the worst writing,
if you're on Twitter and you just experienced

00:13:56.180 --> 00:14:00.810
people, the most extreme versions, you'll
think, well, they're stupid and evil.

00:14:00.810 --> 00:14:07.070
Um, so I think we do need, we do need as individuals
and in a policy process, we do need people

00:14:07.070 --> 00:14:11.140
to propose ideas, other people to say, no,
that won't work because of X. Here's a better

00:14:11.140 --> 00:14:12.140
way.

00:14:12.140 --> 00:14:15.570
And you have to have a process by which the
best ideas when universities and we're supposed

00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:16.570
to do that.

